canonical `kinds` map at the bottom — Valid() and the ID parser read from the map, not the const list, so a missing map entry silently breaks NewID(KindFoo) with ErrUnknownKind. For brand-new tables, services already exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) from migration 0002 so NO companion ALTER TABLE is needed on the parent (UNLIKE BE-0435 api_keys parent). Forward-applicable to any future grant table whose parent IS the leaf of the Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service hierarchy or whose parent is otherwise structurally leaf-like (a hypothetical KindDeploymentGrant / KindJobGrant would inherit this template).
ralph/progress.txt:61:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, lifecycle-projection-chokepoint instance (BE-0408 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_deployment_lifecycle_e2e_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/deployment_lifecycle_e2e_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestDeploymentLifecycleE2ECoversCallSites + TestDeploymentLifecycleE2EPreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Deployment Lifecycle End-to-End Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: deployment lifecycle end-to-end tests (BE-0408)" subsection; THIRTIETH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0407 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestDeploymentLifecycleE2E ./...`, CI step name `Deployment lifecycle end-to-end tests`, verify.sh prefix #32 inserted between BE-0407's #31 and the trailing optionals renumbered #32-#36 → #33-#37 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 36 → # 37` and synthetic-fixture `# 35 → # 36` updated to match, plus BE-0400's trailing-optional commentary `#32-#35 → #33-#36`). Three forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST lifecycle-projection chokepoint (a slog.LogValuer pure projection on a closed-set lifecycle-state-machine row): (1) `slog TEXT AND JSON HANDLERS BOTH EMIT A time= ATTRIBUTE THAT BREAKS DETERMINISTIC ROUND-TRIP ASSERTIONS — INSTALL A ReplaceAttr HOOK THAT DROPS slog.TimeKey AT THE TOP-LEVEL GROUP ONLY (return slog.Attr{} when len(groups)==0 && a.Key==slog.TimeKey)`. Scope the drop to top-level groups so a customer-named attribute called `time` inside the projected group is still asserted on. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a slog.LogValuer and whose canonical pair asserts repeat-call determinism on the rendered slog form. (2) `LIFECYCLE CHOKEPOINTS (closed-set rows whose terminal-vs-non-terminal enum classification implies a database CHECK on a sibling timestamp column) MUST PIN THE DATABASE-CHECK INVARIANT AT THE GO PROJECTION INSIDE THE CANONICAL PAIR's CLOSED-SET SELF-CHECK SEQUENCE`. The deployments_finished_consistent table CHECK becomes self-check #4 here: non-terminal status (queued, running) ⇒ nil FinishedAt; terminal status (succeeded, failed, cancelled, rolled_back) ⇒ set FinishedAt. Otherwise an in-memory regression that violates the CHECK only surfaces against a live Postgres; targeted canonical-pair gates do not. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a row from a table whose closed-set status column has a sibling-column CHECK constraint. (3) `THE STRUCTURAL WHITELIST/BLACKLIST ASSERTION FOR A slog.LogValuer SHOULD DRIVE THROUGH THE JSON HANDLER (not the text handler) SO A REGRESSION THAT SWITCHED THE HANDLER STILL TRIPS THE CONTRACT`. The redaction contract isn't "this text never contains this string" (defeated by an escape change); it's "this group projects these keys and not these keys". JSON-route the projection, json.Unmarshal into map[string]any, assert keys whitelist + blacklist. The text-handler canary still fires for the secret-marker absence canary; both surfaces should be asserted side-by-side. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a slog.LogValuer with a documented field whitelist. One canonical-pair-package gotcha worth pinning forward: `WHEN THE CHOKEPOINT'S EXPORTED API LIVES ON A VALUE RECEIVER OF A TYPE DECLARED IN THE PARENT PACKAGE (here store.Deployment with LogValue() on the value receiver), THE CANONICAL PAIR FILE GOES IN package <parent>_test AND IMPORTS THE PARENT PACKAGE — mirrors BE-0379..BE-0407 convention; no white-box `package store` test needed`. The chokepoint is `package store_test`'s view of `(store.Deployment).LogValue()`, with helper renders (`deploymentLifecycleRenderLogValue`, `deploymentLifecycleStructuredLogValue`) wrapping the slog API. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:62:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pure-function-chokepoint instance (BE-0407 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_service_desired_state_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/service_desired_state_canonical_test.go's new canonical pair TestServiceDesiredStateCoversCallSites + TestServiceDesiredStatePreservesContractUnderContention + SECURITY.md "## Service Desired-State Golden Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: service desired-state golden tests (BE-0407)" subsection; TWENTY-NINTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0406 verification-suite mega-pattern. Canonical command `go test -run TestServiceDesiredState ./...`, CI step name `Service desired-state golden tests`, verify.sh prefix #31 inserted between BE-0406's #30 and the trailing optionals renumbered #31-#35 → #32-#36 in the same edit (with the BE-0400 external-smoke static-test constants `# 35 → # 36` and synthetic-fixture `# 34 → # 35` updated to match). Two forward-applicable extensions specific to the FIRST pure-no-context-no-port chokepoint instance — the chokepoint is `(*dokploy.Renderer).Render(in RenderInput) (RenderedSpec, error)` which is a pure function of its input alone (no context, no I/O, no port interfaces, no Repository): (1) `THE CONTENTION BURST BUILDS A FRESH RENDERER PER ITERATION VIA dokploy.NewRenderer() — NOT A SHARED INSTANCE — BECAUSE CONSTRUCTION IS CHEAP AND THE CONTRACT IS "NewRenderer THEN Render"`. The renderer has no port interfaces at construction time, so a shared instance would also work, but the per-iteration construction pins the documented API contract verbatim and surfaces a regression that smuggled in a sync.Once mutating a per-call map, a sync.Pool reused without resetting, or any cached per-instance view as a per-iteration mismatch even when an aggregate pass count matched. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint is a constructed-instance method (`pkg.NewX().Method(args)` style) where the constructor is cheap and takes no ports: build a fresh chokepoint per iteration via the documented constructor; the burst's value is the assertion that the package's method-internal pure logic has no package-level shared state AND that the documented constructor stays cheap and reusable. (2) `THE VALUE-FREE PROJECTION CANARY SEEDS THE SECRET MARKER INTO EVERY LEVEL OF THE VARIABLE HIERARCHY (org/project/env/service) AND ASSERTS MARKER-ABSENCE IN THREE REDACTED SURFACES: Summary JSON, slog LogValue group, AND Summary.String() form`. Unlike BE-0406's single-surface canary (ProposedDisplay survival + closed-set-tag absence), the renderer's redaction contract spans three concrete projection surfaces — the JSON-marshalled Summary, the slog handler's text output, and the single-line String form — because each is a separate code path the redactor must enforce. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose chokepoint emits a type that implements slog.LogValuer alongside an explicit Summary projection: drive the marker-absence canary through ALL THREE projection surfaces (encoding/json output, slog.NewTextHandler captured output, and any documented String form), not just the JSON; a regression in any one surface ships to a different operator audience (audit pipeline vs structured log handler vs ad-hoc fmt.Println) and pinning all three end-to-end keeps the redaction contract bound to the public API, not just the wire format. One renderer-canonical gotcha worth pinning forward: `domain.ID FIXTURES IN THE dokploy_test PACKAGE MUST USE A 26-CHAR CROCKFORD-BASE32-LOWERCASE SUFFIX EXCLUDING i, l, o, u; the canonical pair uses serviceDesiredStateFixedID(domain.KindXxx, last byte ∈ {a, b, c, d}) — last bytes from the safe set keep determinism self-check #6's reflect.DeepEqual stable across runs without invented IDs that LOOK 26-char-valid but carry excluded letters. The canonical pair file lives in `package dokploy_test` (mirrors BE-0406's `package migrateimport_test`) — the chokepoint is exported (Render is a public method on Renderer) so the canonical pair stays in the _test package next to renderer_test.go rather than the package-internal store_test convention used by the BE-0404 break-glass pair. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:73:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, pagination-stability instance (BE-0397 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_pagination_stability_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/pagination/pagination_stability_test.go's new canonical pair TestPaginationStabilityCoversCallSites + TestPaginationStabilityPreservesPagesUnderInserts + SECURITY.md "## Pagination Stability Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: pagination stability tests (BE-0397)" subsection; NINETEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0396 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestPaginationStability ./...`, CI step name `Pagination stability tests`, verify.sh prefix #22 inserted between BE-0396's #21 and the trailing optionals renumbered 22->23/23->24/24->25/25->26 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0396's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after the previous required gate), (d) SECURITY.md section substring set (here 19 substrings: internal/controlplane/pagination, pagination_stability_test.go, deterministic, fakeStore, concurrentPaginationStore, EncodeCursor, DecodeCursor, base64url, no duplicates, no drops, request_id, yalla.output.v1, yalla.error.v1, tenant isolation, the two function names, Every push and PR, redacted, and the self-locating static-test filename), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0397-specific extensions to the verification-suite template, all forward-applicable to ANY future verification story whose subject is a list-pagination / cursor-encoding / wire-shape invariant in a Go package with an existing single-call test fixture (BE-0398 tenant isolation, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal): (1) `DUAL-FIXTURE REUSE — EXISTING ZERO-MUTEX SINGLE-CALL FIXTURE FOR THE COVERAGE MEMBER, A FILE-LOCAL MUTEX-GUARDED FIXTURE FOR THE BURST MEMBER` — the canonical pair lives in `package pagination` (the same internal package as the existing `page_test.go`) so the coverage member reuses the existing `fakeStore` / `row` / `positionByID` / `rowID` helpers verbatim. The contention burst introduces a SECOND in-file store, `concurrentPaginationStore`, with a `sync.RWMutex`-guarded rows slice and `insert` / `delete` / `list` / `listAsc` methods — declaring it in the new test file (not in `page_test.go`) keeps the existing `fakeStore`'s zero-mutex shape for the single-call unit tests that rely on it. (2) `WIDEN THE EXISTING FIXTURE WITH AN ASCENDING SIBLING METHOD WHEN THE COVERAGE SCENARIO TABLE NEEDS BOTH DIRECTIONS — KEEP THE DESCENDING-DEFAULT METHOD UNCHANGED` — the existing `fakeStore.list` hard-codes descending order (matches the audit-log / deployment-list canonical case). Rather than refactor it (and risk regressing the existing TestCursor* tests), add a sibling `listAsc` method on the same receiver in the new test file so the closed-set coverage member can iterate both directions through a `storeLike` interface (`list` + `listAsc`) without forking the descending fixture. The interface lives in the new test file so the existing `fakeStore` definition file is untouched. (3) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE COVERS EVERY DISTINCT PAGINATION SHAPE — EMPTY / SINGLE-ROW / EXACT-PAGE / MULTI-PAGE-NO-REMAINDER / MULTI-PAGE-PARTIAL-TRAILING / SMALL-PAGE / BOTH DIRECTIONS` — the scenario-table-rejects-duplicate-names self-check at the head of the coverage member guards against a future addition that quietly drops the closed-set property. Each scenario walks BuildPage end-to-end with a safety-bound terminator (1000-page bound) so the test fails loudly if a regression introduces a non-terminating cursor stream; the diagnostic surfaces both the scenario name AND the page index AND the row id of the divergence so an operator can correlate the gate failure with a specific in-flight traversal without re-running the suite locally. The contention burst sizing (workers=4, iterations=8, seedRows=50, pageSize=5) is the smallest power-of-two-burst-table-pair where a non-thread-safe store fails under -race ~99% of the time while keeping the wall-clock under 1s on CI.
ralph/progress.txt:76:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Postgres-disconnects instance (BE-0394 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_postgres_disconnects_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/chaos_postgres_disconnects_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsCoversCallSites + TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsMapsToTypedUnavailableEnvelope + new internal/controlplane/store/fakepg package (Server + DisconnectFault + NewServer) + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Postgres Disconnects" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Postgres disconnects (BE-0394)" subsection; SIXTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0393 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosPostgresDisconnects ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Postgres disconnects`, verify.sh prefix #19 inserted between BE-0393's #18 and the trailing optionals renumbered 19->20/20->21/21->22/22->23 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0393's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0394 substring count = 16; matches BE-0393's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0394-specific extensions to the chaos-classification template, all forward-applicable to ANY future chaos-Postgres / chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit sibling whose subject is the store layer or any pgxpool-backed dependency: (1) `NEW IN-PROCESS FAKE NETWORK ENDPOINT FOR PGX` — unlike BE-0393's dokployfake (which exists as a long-standing HTTP test double under internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake), there was no fakepg before this story. The minimal viable shape is a TCP listener on 127.0.0.1:0 that does NOT speak the Postgres wire protocol — it accepts the TCP connection (so pgx's dial completes successfully — important: graceful FIN, NOT RST via SetLinger(0), because RST races the dial on some kernels and surfaces as `connection reset by peer` on connect rather than the production failure mode of "dial succeeds, handshake fails") and then closes the conn, so pgx observes EOF on its startup-handshake read. The fake exposes a FIFO Fault queue (only DisconnectFault for now), a Host()/Port()/Addr() trio for DSN construction, an AcceptedConnections() counter so the harness can assert pgx made >=1 TCP attempt per op (a regression that short-circuited pgx without ever attempting a network connect would otherwise pass silently), Close() (idempotent), New(testing.TB) for tests inside the testing package, and NewServer() error for worker-goroutine constructors that cannot satisfy testing.TB. Forward-applicable to every future chaos-Postgres sibling: add SQLErrorFault (writes an ErrorResponse before closing — would require pgproto3 dependency from pgx), LatencyFault (delays the close), AuthFailFault (writes a FATAL ErrorResponse during startup). (2) `CLASSIFIER-WRAP CONTRACT, NOT END-TO-END CALLSITE WRAP` — unlike BE-0393's chaos test which exercises real production methods on the typed Dokploy client (c.GetDeployment, c.EnsureOrganization, c.RemoveService) so the test asserts on errors the client's PRODUCTION wrapping chokepoint already produced, BE-0394's chaos test exercises raw pgxpool ops (Pool.Ping, Pool.Acquire, Pool.Begin, Pool.Exec, Pool.Query) and EXPLICITLY wraps the resulting pgx error through apierr.StoreUnavailable inside the runtime member before asserting the contract. This is because the store layer does not currently route every call site through a single classifier chokepoint the way the Dokploy client does — store call sites return raw pgx errors and the wrapping happens at handler-shim boundaries. The chaos contract therefore pins the wrapper's behaviour given realistic pgx-derived errors, not every call site's wrapping behaviour. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose subject lacks a single production classifier chokepoint: assert the wrapper given realistic causes, not the production path. When a future story (BE-04xx) adds a store-layer classifier chokepoint, the chaos test can switch to invoking that chokepoint instead — the scenario table and the redaction/dependency/code assertions stay identical. (3) `ASYMMETRIC REDACTION CONTRACT — PASSWORD AT EVERY LEVEL, USERNAME AT WRAPPER ONLY` — pgx's connect error formatter intentionally includes the DSN username in error messages (for operator debuggability) but redacts the password. The chaos test's chaosPostgresSecretMarkers (cause-chain scan) therefore lists ONLY the password sentinel; the wrapper-envelope assertion (chaosPostgresEnvelopeMarkers) lists BOTH the password and the username sentinel because apierr.StoreUnavailable's rendered message is static ("the Yalla datastore is temporarily unavailable") and MUST NOT echo ANY pgx cause-chain content. The runtime member walks errors.Unwrap chain for the password marker AND asserts the wrapper envelope's rendered Error() string for both markers. Forward-applicable: any future chaos test whose subject is a wire-protocol library that includes debug info in errors should split the redaction contract into chain-level (real secrets only) + envelope-level (anything pgx-style libraries might leak into debug strings). The sentinel-literal approach (chaosPostgresSentinelPassword="yallachaossecret_DO_NOT_LEAK_42") keeps the assertion meaningful: if the wrapper EVER started embedding the cause's message, the sentinel would surface and the gate would trip on the first observation. Per-iteration setup cost is bounded by chaosPostgresConnectTimeout=750ms (per-attempt pgx connect deadline) which is strictly less than chaosPostgresOpTimeout=2s (per-op context deadline), so the burst (5 scenarios × 4 workers × 3 iterations = 60 iterations) completes in ~0.5s wall-clock.
ralph/progress.txt:82:- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, fake-Dokploy-contract instance (BE-0386 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_fake_dokploy_contract_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake/dokployfake_test.go's new canonical pair TestFakeDokployContractDeterministicHierarchyIDs + TestFakeDokployContractRecordedRequestsRedactCredentials + SECURITY.md "## Fake Dokploy Contract Tests" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: fake Dokploy contract tests (BE-0386)" subsection; EIGHTH worked instance of the BE-0379/BE-0380/BE-0381/BE-0382/BE-0383/BE-0384/BE-0385 verification-suite mega-pattern, confirms the BE-0385 closing-line prediction held verbatim — canonical command `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...`, CI step name `Fake Dokploy contract tests`, verify.sh prefix #12). Four BE-0386-specific extensions to the template: (1) `CANONICAL FILE = CONTRACT-OWNING PACKAGE, NOT A CONSUMER` — unlike BE-0385 which pinned `internal/controlplane/worker/queue_test.go` (the worker's lease test) and BE-0384 which pinned the quota test in the quota package, BE-0386's canonical file is `internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake/dokployfake_test.go` — the FAKE's own contract tests, not a downstream consumer's tests against the fake. The rationale: a fake-X contract-test story should bind to the package whose contract is being asserted (the fake itself), not to a caller that happens to use the fake. The worker test in `internal/controlplane/worker/dokployfake_test.go` (TestWorkerRunnerProvisionsThroughFakeDokploy, TestWorkerRunnerSurfacesFakeDokployFaults) is downstream evidence, NOT the canonical contract. Forward-applicable: any future verification story whose subject is a deterministic test fixture (BE-0392 load smoke harness, BE-0393 chaos test harness, BE-0399 backup-restore rehearsal) should pin the fixture's own contract tests, not a consumer's. (2) `-RUN FILTER PRE-EXISTING IN PRD BUT MATCHED ZERO TESTS` — sibling of BE-0382's `EXISTING-TEST-SATISFIES-RUN-FILTER` but the OPPOSITE shape: the PRD's `verificationLoop.requiredBackendCommands` already contained `go test -run TestFakeDokploy ./...` before BE-0386 landed, BUT the `TestFakeDokploy` prefix matched ZERO existing tests anywhere in the repo (the dokployfake package's tests are TestCreateHierarchy…, TestTwoServers…, TestRequestRecordingRedactsCredentials, etc. — none start with TestFakeDokploy). The named CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING gates would have silently passed against zero tests. BE-0386 adds value by (a) CREATING the canonical TestFakeDokploy* pair so the `-run` filter binds to real assertions, (b) wiring CI/verify.sh/CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY/AGENTS surfaces as named gates, (c) adding the static defence that pins both halves. Forward-applicable: when picking up ANY verification-suite story whose PRD `-run` filter exists, FIRST run `go test -run <Prefix> ./...` — if it shows `no tests to run` everywhere, the story MUST add canonical tests under the prefix BEFORE wiring the gates, otherwise the gates pass vacuously. (3) `CANONICAL-PAIR DUAL-INVARIANT NAMING — DETERMINISM × RECORDER-REDACTION` — TestFakeDokployContractDeterministicHierarchyIDs pins the per-server deterministic ID allocator (two independent dokployfake.New() servers driven through the same org/project/env/app/deployment chain mint byte-identical org_1/proj_1/env_1/app_1/dep_1, with per-server request counts isolated to 5/5); TestFakeDokployContractRecordedRequestsRedactCredentials pins the recorder hot path that swaps the Authorization header for output.Sentinel AND scans the body for the bearer literal (3 recorded requests across happy-path create / GET read-back / failure-path POST whose body deliberately echoes the bearer — each MUST have AuthHeader==Sentinel, Headers[Authorization]==Sentinel, no body retains the bearer literal). The SECURITY.md substring set MUST include `dokployfake.New` (constructor — deletion breaks every worker test), `Server.Requests` (recorder seam), `output.Sentinel` (the redaction substitute — a different sentinel would silently fork the redaction contract), AND `TestLiveDokploySmoke` (the AC2 opt-in-external escape hatch's test name) so auditors verify the contract from documentation alone. Substring count raised to 15 (vs BE-0385's 13). (4) `VERIFY.SH RENUMBER OF OPTIONALS BLOCK — SEVEN INSERTIONS NOW (#6→#12)` — inserting required step #12 between #11 (job worker lease) and the optionals renumbered 12→13/13→14/14→15/15→16 in the same edit (govulncheck/staticcheck/golangci-lint/goreleaser). Now SEVEN consecutive required-step insertions with optional renumbering; the next contributor adding required step #13 (BE-0387 redaction tests, canonical `go test -run TestRedaction ./...` if PRD names that filter) should follow the same edit shape AND prepare for an optional-band renumber. Per-story knobs for siblings: same as BE-0385's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and 13-15-entry substring set (per-suite invariants — preserve closed-set discipline), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers (which seam each canonical-pair function pins). Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six.
ralph/progress.txt:146:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface a constraint violation whose error message names the constraint); LogValueRedactsCiphertextAndPlaintext (a structured log record that captures the variable struct redacts BOTH plaintext AND ciphertext to output.Sentinel while exposing provider id + key id). (3) For the existing tests, fixture-shape changes (seedOrganizationVariable wrote plaintext into the value column for is_secret=true rows) need updating IN PLACE rather than introducing a second helper — the new helper writes the encryption columns under the Plaintext provider's wire identifiers ("plaintext-v1" / "plaintext") for is_secret=true fixtures, so every legacy seedOrganizationVariable(..., true) call site keeps working. (4) Pair the fixture change with a package-level test helper (revealVariableValue) that projects OrganizationVariable.Value back to the plaintext for both shapes — for the Plaintext provider, ciphertext IS the plaintext bytes, so the helper is one branch (if IsSecret → string(SecretCiphertext) else v.Value). Tests that previously asserted v.Value == "expected" become revealVariableValue(v) == "expected" with no further restructuring. (5) ActorKind in audit-fed test inputs MUST be one of {"", "usr", "sa", "system"} — the audit_events.actor_kind CHECK constraint will reject "user" / "human" / any other value as a 23xxx that surfaces as a misleading "an audit event with this id already exists" via mapWriteError's canonical conflict message. The actual diagnostic is the actor_kind CHECK, not duplicate id. (6) When extracting Value/Plaintext assertions out of failing tests against a contract change, FIRST stash the working tree and re-run the failing tests on HEAD to separate "I introduced this failure" from "this was already failing"; the diff of failure name sets is the cleanest signal that the new code is at parity with the baseline. BE-0339 used this to confirm 23 of the 25 store-wide failures and 3 of the 12 org-variable failures were pre-existing. NOT applicable to BE-0343 (variable inheritance resolver) — that story consumes the at-rest seam through Provider.Open against multiple scopes simultaneously and needs its own pattern around the merge order + per-scope Open call site. NOT applicable to BE-0351 (secret encryption key rotation) — that story consumes the partial index organization_variables_secret_routing_idx + the KeyIDs() / ActiveKeyID() surface to enumerate rows referencing a retired key and re-Seal them, but the rotation worker is a separate operational concern from the at-rest write path this pattern owns.
ralph/progress.txt:151:- Real-quota concurrent oversubscription integration test (BE-0323 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/project_quota_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0324..0338 — every other quota-dimension PRD story that needs to prove "parallel requests cannot oversubscribe the quota" against the dimension's owning Service.Create call): one Go test, t.Parallel, against testutil.RequireMigratedDB; wires a REAL *quota.Checker over a REAL *store.QuotaRepository + quota.StaticPlanResolver, and a REAL *store.<Resource>Service + REAL *store.AuditRepository, with the only fakes being the Authorizer and JobEnqueuer (recordingAuthorizer / recordingJobs from projectservice_test.go) which always succeed because their failure-path stories are covered elsewhere. Six load-bearing rules: (1) pre-seed quota_usage with used_value=0 for (org, dimension) BEFORE the race so the SELECT FOR UPDATE inside Checker.LockUsage acquires an existing row rather than racing inserts — the lock contract under test is row-lock serialisation, not row-creation; (2) seed quota_policies as scope_kind='plan_default' (not 'organization' override) so the plan-default fallback path inside QuotaRepository.EffectiveLimit is the one exercised — the org-override path has its own dedicated repository test (TestQuotaRepositoryEffectiveLimitPrefersOrgOverride); (3) launch attempts > limit goroutines (BE-0323 uses 20 attempts / limit 5 — 4:1 ratio gives a comfortable margin against any future pool-size or scheduling change without inflating runtime past ~0.5s); each goroutine generates its OWN canonical resource id via domain.MustNewID + a unique canonical slug fmt.Sprintf("p-%02d", i) so a slug conflict cannot disguise itself as a quota rejection; (4) assert successes == limit AND rejections == attempts - limit exactly, NOT >= / <= — the lock contract guarantees an exact split, and a "successes >= limit / <= attempts" range would silently let a regression through; (5) for EVERY rejection, assert all five ExceededDetail fields (Resource, Limit, Requested=1, Reserved==limit, Current==0) because at the moment the lock yields to a losing transaction every winning reservation is already committed — anything less than Reserved==limit means the lock did not serialise — and assert the diagnostic string never echoes any caller input (the i-indexed slug, display name, request id, or correlation id) so a future logging path cannot turn the error into a content channel; (6) post-race assertions cover three independent surfaces of the same invariant: raw count of projects rows for the org (must equal limit), raw count of active quota_reservations rows for the dimension (must equal limit), AND quotaRepo.SumActiveReservations through Store.Read (must equal limit) — the third surface catches a future regression where the table content diverges from the repository's view. Fake-call assertions pin step order at the unit-of-work level: authz.calls == attempts (every transaction reaches authorization) but jobs.calls == limit (rejected transactions never reach the JobEnqueuer because quota fails first and rolls back the whole tx). The dimension constant comes from the public store.QuotaResource* surface (BE-0323 uses store.QuotaResourceProjects) so a future rename surfaces as a compile error rather than a flaky test. NOT applicable to quota dimensions whose enforcement seam lives outside the *Service.Create unit of work (e.g. cpu_millicores / memory_mb / storage_gb where the dimension is summed across child resources — those need a different shape that exercises the dimension's actual enforcement gate). The CapDeploy concurrent_deployments dimension is the load-bearing precedent for "summed across rows" enforcement (DeploymentService.Create at deployment_service.go:209+691) and would clone this template by swapping the Service + dimension while keeping the six rules intact.
ralph/progress.txt:169:- environment.read-on-bare-{environment_id}-resolver policy matrix (BE-0156, internal/controlplane/httpapi/environments_get_policy_test.go) is the structural sibling of the {project_id}-resolver siblings (BE-0144 env.read on the variables route, BE-0150 environment.read on the parent-scoped route) for endpoints that use a BARE-ID resolver (environmentIDResolver, future serviceIDResolver / deploymentIDResolver). TWO load-bearing distinctions from the projectIDResolver siblings. (1) The resolver pins NO ProjectID leg on the resource scope (`policy.Scope{OrganizationID: principal.home, EnvironmentID: r.PathValue("environment_id")}`), so the engine's one-way Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) denies EVERY scoped grant at the boundary via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope — project-scoped, env-scoped, AND service-scoped grants all DENY on this route, even when the grant names the target environment's own id or the target environment's own parent project, because the grant scope pins ProjectID and the resource scope does not. The acceptance criteria's three containment properties (sibling project not reached / staging-grant-doesn't-imply-production / service-grant-shields-parent-env-secrets) are still pinned at the engine against their natural resources (project / env / service Resource), but the wire tie-in INVERTS the projectIDResolver shape: instead of "project-scoped grant on target ALLOWS, sibling DENIES," every scoped-key type DENIES with ReasonDeniedOutOfScope on this route and only org-wide roles + the org-level grant allow. The org-level Viewer grant remains the load-bearing ALLOW because its grant scope pins nothing past OrganizationID — covers() matches a resource without ProjectID. Pin a "grant on the SAME environment id as the path names" engine-level deny too, so a future relaxation of covers() (e.g. admitting `Scope{EnvironmentID: env_X}` to cover `Resource{Scope{EnvironmentID: env_X}}` regardless of ProjectID) cannot land without failing this test. (2) The support cross-tenant exception is suppressed on the wire by construction — environmentIDResolver pins OrganizationID to principal.home — so the support cross-tenant ALLOW only fires at the engine layer against a hand-built foreign-org Resource. The wire-level "wrong organization" probe surfaces as 404 E_NOT_FOUND from the tenant-scoped GetByID query (never 403, which would confirm existence), and the body must not echo the foreign org id. The contract-test helper bakes in a single canonical principal (getEnvironmentHandlerFor takes `t, reader` only, with authForEnvGet's hardcoded principal), so this matrix introduces a per-test identity-injected helper LOCAL to the policy file (getEnvironmentByIDHandlerForIdentity, signature `(id auth.Identity, authErr error, reader EnvironmentReader) http.Handler`) modelled on listProjectsHandlerFor / listProjectEnvironmentsHandlerFor — adding the helper locally avoids modifying the 9 contract tests that already use the simpler form. ACTION-CONSTANT gotcha to pin: routes.go:574 sets `RequiredAction: string(policy.ActionEnvironmentRead)` for this route, NOT `policy.ActionEnvRead` — both are CapRead per catalog.go but they're different catalog entries (env-vars vs env entity); mixing them up silently swaps which catalog row a future tier-upgrade watches. Forward-applicable to every future bare-id matrix on a deep resource (service.read on /v1/services/{service_id}, deployment.read on /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}, etc).
ralph/progress.txt:212:- Provisioning jobs (0008_provisioning_jobs, store/job.go): the durable, idempotent queue of Dokploy provisioning work. The provisioning_jobs table is tenant-scoped (organization_id FK + composite (organization_id, project_id|environment_id|service_id) FKs so a job can never target another tenant's resource), idempotent (UNIQUE (organization_id, idempotency_key)), and exposes UNIQUE (organization_id, id) as a composite-FK target — 0008 also wires the FK that 0006 deferred (quota_reservations.job_id -> provisioning_jobs). DB CHECKs enforce the closed status set, "terminal status iff finished_at set", "lease held iff status=running", and attempts<=max_attempts; the Go state machine (store.JobStatus.CanTransitionTo, backed by the jobTransitions map: queued->running|cancelled, running->succeeded|retrying|failed|dead_letter|cancelled, retrying->running|cancelled, terminals have no edges) is the authoritative validator. JobRepository follows the ProjectRepository pattern (mutations need *Tx, reads take Querier, NotFound for cross-tenant ids); Transition locks the row FOR UPDATE, validates the edge before any write (illegal edge = apierr.Conflict), and derives lease/attempt/timestamp bookkeeping from the target status so a caller cannot leave a CHECK-violating row. error_summary is run through an output.NewRedactor() backstop on every write (Insert + Transition) — the store IS the redaction chokepoint for job errors, unlike audit metadata which the service redacts.
ralph/progress.txt:216:- Postgres-backed worker queue (store/job.go ClaimNext, worker/queue.go): the durable queue has two halves. store.JobRepository.ClaimNext is the SKIP LOCKED claim — SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED picks the single most-eligible job (queued|retrying AND next_run_at<=now, OR running AND lease_deadline<now) so concurrent claimers skip a locked row instead of blocking, then transitions it to running inside the same Store.Write tx. An expired-lease job is reclaimed THROUGH the state machine (running->retrying->running, so attempt/lease bookkeeping stays consistent); a job past its retry budget is dead-lettered instead and the poll claims nothing. worker/queue.go is the store-backed Claimer/Lease impl: loop.go stays pure (interfaces only, fake-tested) and queue.go is the only worker file that imports store. Failure classification: worker.Terminal(err)/IsTerminal mark a permanent failure (-> failed, never retried); a plain error is transient (-> retrying with worker.Backoff bounded exponential delay, or -> dead_letter when Attempts>=MaxAttempts). StoreClaimer.complete() records every outcome on a context.WithoutCancel + timeout context — once the runner ran, the outcome write must survive shutdown; only an interrupted (un-run) job returns non-nil from Lease.Run so the loop releases it. The JobRunner (real Dokploy provisioner) is still injected/faked; cmd/yalla-worker stays on NoopClaimer until that runner lands.
ralph/progress.txt:217:- Fake Dokploy (internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake): the default Dokploy for EVERY normal test — a live Dokploy server is an opt-in external smoke test only. dokployfake.New() returns a *Server backed by httptest serving a Dokploy-shaped REST API (organization/project/environment/application/compose/database/domain/deployment/logs/status). It mimics Dokploy's own JSON shapes, NOT yalla.output.v1/yalla.error.v1 — it stands in for the upstream provisioning API, not a Yalla public surface (so the "stable envelope" acceptance criterion is N/A for Dokploy-facing test doubles). IDs are deterministic per-kind counters (org_1, proj_1, ...). Server.QueueFault arms a FIFO fault queue (StatusFault for 4xx/5xx, TimeoutFault honouring request cancellation, MalformedJSONFault); each request consumes one fault, applied before auth+routing. Every request is recorded (Server.Requests()) with Authorization/X-Api-Key/X-Auth-Token/Cookie header values replaced by output.Sentinel and the body run through an output.Redactor seeded with the bearer token — so a worker test can prove credentials were sent without the secret ever entering the fixture. The package depends only on internal/output + stdlib (no testing.TB, no store), so any package's tests import it without a cycle.
ralph/progress.txt:218:- Typed Dokploy client (internal/controlplane/dokploy.Client): customer/handler code NEVER calls Dokploy — only worker/provisioner code, through this typed wrapper. The client exposes Yalla intents (EnsureProject/EnsureService/DeployService/ReadDeploymentLogs/RemoveService/...), not raw Dokploy ops. The Dokploy token is injected via Config.Token, held unexported, exposed by no method, and redacted by Client.LogValue. "Ensure" intents are idempotent against Yalla's source of truth: an optional ExistingID does GET+verify, an empty one does POST+create (the worker records the returned Dokploy ID in Postgres). Only idempotent methods (GET, DELETE) are retried — never POST — and only on apierr.Retryable errors, with bounded exponential backoff; each attempt gets a per-request context timeout and forwards X-Request-Id/X-Correlation-Id from telemetry.FromContext. Every failure maps onto the apierr taxonomy (400->Invalid, 401/403->Internal as a non-retryable Yalla misconfig, 404->NotFound, 409->Conflict, 429/5xx/transport->DokployUnavailable, deadline->Timeout(DependencyDokploy)); the redacted upstream body is wrapped only as the log-only cause, never a client-facing Message. Normal tests run against dokployfake; the client touches no Postgres.
ralph/progress.txt:224:- Drift reconciliation is internal/controlplane/reconcile: a pure engine that classifies every Yalla<->Dokploy divergence into exactly one of three buckets — DriftSafe (auto-repaired through the Repairer port: env var ensures/updates/removals, re-bound managed domains), DriftDangerous (recorded through the ReviewRecorder port: missing managed service, missing managed database, renamed domain on a known Dokploy ID, service type changed), or DriftUnmanaged (a Dokploy resource without a Yalla counterpart, recorded through the UnmanagedRecorder port — never auto-deleted and never exposed to customers by default). Diff(desired, actual) is a pure function; the Reconciler depends only on the four port interfaces (DesiredStateReader, ActualStateReader, Repairer, ReviewRecorder, UnmanagedRecorder) so adapters land in store/worker/dokploy without reaching back into reconcile. Every classification field carries a stable, value-free DriftReason (env_var_changed, domain_renamed, service_missing, database_missing, resource_unmanaged, ...) — an env var value reaches Action only via DesiredValue, consumed by the Repairer adapter and nowhere else. Apply is best-effort: per-action failures land in Result.Failures with their error string scrubbed through output.Redactor; the loop only stops on ctx cancellation. Untyped adapter errors are upgraded to apierr.StoreUnavailable (desired) or apierr.DokployUnavailable (actual); typed apierr.* from adapters (NotFound, Forbidden) flow through unchanged so callers keep the real taxonomy.
ralph/progress.txt:245:- Bare top-level lookup-by-id endpoint for a deep resource (BE-0154 pattern, GET /v1/environments/{environment_id}, store/environment.go GetByID + GetEnvironment, httpapi/environments.go): a customer-facing route that addresses a deep resource (env, service, deployment) by its global id WITHOUT carrying its parent ids in the path. Two load-bearing constraints: (1) the resolver builds policy.Resource{Scope: {OrganizationID = principal.home, <ResourceLevel>ID = path.id}} — pinning OrgID to the principal's home (NEVER the caller-supplied id) is the structural anti-spoof, and pinning the resource-level id is the only scope leg the path actually carries; (2) the resolver CANNOT pin the parent ids without a DB lookup, and the policy engine's Scope.covers() rule (internal/controlplane/policy/policy.go) requires every level the grant pins to match the resource — a grant with pinned ProjectID against a resource with empty ProjectID is structurally denied. Accepted design: middleware admits org-wide read roles + org-wide grants + (for CapRead actions) the support cross-tenant exception is suppressed because the resolver pins OrgID to principal.home; project-/env-/service-scoped grants are denied at the boundary by design, and grant-only principals must use the parent-scoped /v1/projects/{project_id}/environments instead. Document this in the route description, the resolver doc, and the handler doc — readers will misinterpret the structural deny as a bug otherwise. The store layer adds a tenant-scoped GetByID(ctx, q, organizationID, resourceID) on the existing repository (composite (organization_id, id) predicate so cross-tenant returns apierr.NotFound, never another tenant's row, never a 500 leaking the cause), and the existing reader struct grows a Get<Resource>(ctx, organizationID, resourceID) method that wraps GetByID inside Store.Read. The HTTP layer adds a NEW narrow port (distinct from the existing parent-scoped port — e.g. EnvironmentReader vs ProjectEnvironmentReader — so a handler change to one doesn't ripple through the other), a NEW resolver function (e.g. environmentIDResolver), and a NEW handler that REUSES the existing wire-shape projection (projectEnvironmentOf for environments) so the GET-by-id response is byte-identical to one element of the LIST response (modulo the wrapping object name). cmd/yalla-api/main.go passes the same *store.<Resource>Reader value twice — once for the parent-scoped port, once for the new bare-lookup port — because both methods live on the same store struct; no second startup wiring is needed. The contract tests cover the standard envelope: happy path, NotFound (cross-tenant + unknown both render as the same 404 — no oracle), 401 unauthenticated + invalid credentials (no token echo), 503 store outage (no raw cause leak), 500 nil-port wiring guard, envelope shape lock (asserts the documented field set against a generic decode so a future struct column added without updating the projection is caught), bearer-token never-logged guard (slog over a buffer), OpenAPI route registration, and TWO resolver unit tests (pins home org + path id; no panic with no principal on context). The matching policy matrix story (BE-0156 for environments, the structural template for service/deployment lookups) tests the deny — the deny matrix on the bare endpoint is part of the contract, not a gap.
ralph/progress.txt:249:- Bare-id route on a deployment (BE-0211+0212+0213 pattern, GET /v1/deployments/{deployment_id}, store/deployment.go DeploymentReader.GetServiceDeployment, httpapi/deployments.go DeploymentGetter port + deploymentIDResolver + getServiceDeploymentHandler + getServiceDeploymentPayload, routes.go GET /v1/deployments/{deployment_id} entry, server.go NewHandler DeploymentGetter parameter, cmd/yalla-api/main.go wires the SAME *store.DeploymentReader as BOTH DeploymentLister AND DeploymentGetter): canonical shape for ALL bare-id read endpoints on a Deployment-level deep resource. Two LOAD-BEARING structural differences from the environment / service bare-id routes (BE-0154 / its services sibling): (1) policy.Scope has NO DeploymentID leg — the Scope hierarchy stops at ServiceID (Organization -> Project -> Environment -> Service), so the resolver pins ONLY OrganizationID, NOT (org + DeploymentID) the way environmentIDResolver pins (org + EnvironmentID). The resource scope carries no inner leg at all, which makes the engine's covers() rule deny EVERY scoped grant (project/env/service) at the boundary via ReasonDeniedOutOfScope — even a service-scoped grant naming the deployment's actual parent service is denied, because covers() is one-way (a grant scope that pins ServiceID cannot cover a resource scope that does not). Principals whose only access is a scoped grant must use a parent-scoped route family to address a deployment. (2) The store-layer adapter intentionally skips the parent-prefetch the list adapter performs: DeploymentReader.GetServiceDeployment composes only DeploymentRepository.GetByID inside a short-lived Store.Read tx (no service prefetch), because the schema's composite foreign keys on the deployments table already guarantee (organization_id, project_id), (organization_id, environment_id), and (organization_id, service_id) cannot diverge across the deployment row — a tenant-scoped GetByID is itself a complete tenant-isolation check, and a prefetch would only duplicate the work of the schema. This is the load-bearing distinction from the list adapter ListServiceDeployments (which DOES prefetch the service to disambiguate "service missing" from "service has no deployments"). The wire shape (serviceDeployment + serviceDeploymentOf in service_deployments.go) is REUSED verbatim — an agent that listed deployments for a service does not re-parse a different shape to fetch one of them by id. Production wiring passes the same *store.DeploymentReader pointer twice (deploymentService, deploymentReader, deploymentReader, breakGlassService, ...): one concrete adapter satisfies BOTH the DeploymentLister AND DeploymentGetter port interfaces, so adding a second read port costs zero new constructors / dependencies. The contract test suite (deployments_get_test.go, 12 tests) and policy matrix (deployments_get_policy_test.go, 4 parent tests) follow the BE-0210 list-side template structure verbatim, with one structural extension in the grant-containment matrix: a service-scoped grant naming THIS deployment's actual parent service is also denied at the wire, because the bare-id resource scope has no ServiceID leg to match. Future bare-id read endpoints on deep resources whose Scope leg does not exist (deployments today; jobs / runs in later stories) should adopt this exact pattern.
ralph/progress.txt:255:- POST create endpoint for a service-scoped child resource (BE-0250+0251+0252 pattern, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups, mirrors the service_domains POST trio BE-0238+0239+0240; clonable for BE-0264 previews, BE-0289 import previews, and any future bare-id child create): the store-layer composition is the canonical create-unit-of-work shape — ServiceXxxRepository.Insert (single-statement INSERT...RETURNING + mapWriteError mapping driver errors to typed apierr.Conflict whose message is value-free) + ServiceXxxService.Create orchestrator that opens a Store.Write Tx, runs the tenant-scoped parent existence check first (so a cross-tenant or unknown parent id surfaces as a deterministic apierr.NotFound, NEVER a 500 or silent success), runs an in-tx Authorize (defence-in-depth against grant-change races between the HTTP authorize and the desired-state write — the ONLY operation that runs in-tx Authorize; the in-transaction Authorizer is reserved for Create), runs the Insert, and Appends the immutable audit record — all inside the same *Tx so a row can never be persisted without its audit trail. Validation runs BEFORE the Tx is opened (validateCreateXxxInput against a shared validate.Collector so a request rejecting on more than one field surfaces every violation in one typed apierr.InvalidInput; submitted values NEVER appear in violation reasons). The customer-facing create pins any lifecycle status column to a fixed initial value (e.g. ServiceBackupStatusPending) so a customer cannot forge a 'succeeded' / 'completed' status through the public API surface — the worker is the authority for closed-taxonomy transitions; the status column is NOT in the createXxxRequest decoder shape and the validator overwrites in.Status in the validated struct. The audit Metadata records ONLY fields the application layer can prove redaction-safe by construction: structural identifiers (service_id), closed-taxonomy enums (status, certificate_type), and bounded primitives (retention_count, version, enabled flag rendered as "true"/"false"); caller-supplied free text (display_name, schedule, hostname even — schedule is a cron-style expression but is still caller-controlled bytes) is NEVER recorded verbatim, because a customer pasting a token into one of those fields is exactly the footgun a defensive convention avoids. The httpapi-layer composition is the canonical create-handler shape — createXxxRequest decoder (Enabled / boolean-default-true fields are pointers to preserve the absent-vs-explicit-false distinction at the JSON seam — `*bool` decoded; an omitted field collapses to the default at the handler boundary, an explicit `false` rides through unchanged; integer fields take their schema-side default when zero) + createXxxPayload + createXxxHandler that gates on RequireAuth + the typed-internal nil-creator guard + validate.DecodeJSON (strict, max-body-bytes, unknown-field rejection, no input echo on parse failure) + creator.Create + http.StatusCreated yalla.output.v1 envelope projecting through serviceXxxOf. Action is CapWrite (ActionXxxCreate -> CapWrite in catalog.go): owner / admin / developer allowed by role; CI / viewer / support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — load-bearing distinction: a CapDeploy sibling action (e.g. ActionBackupRun, ActionDeploymentCreate) WOULD admit CI. The wire route uses serviceIDResolver (no parent_project_id leg pinned on the resource scope), so EVERY project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied at the boundary via the engine's one-way covers() rule (ReasonDeniedOutOfScope); only an organization-wide write role or an org-level write grant reaches the handler. Cross-tenant: the resolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org id (never the path service's tenant), so cross-tenant service_ids hit the persistence layer with the home org id and surface as a deterministic apierr.NotFound — engine cross-tenant deny is pinned at the engine level (defence-in-depth: EVERY role including Support is denied cross-tenant for CapWrite — the support cross-tenant exception covers only CapRead / CapSupport). Contract test surface: success with full plumbing assertions (principal home org id forwarded, path service id forwarded, actor id forwarded, no caller-body org id trusted), boolean-default-true and explicit-false at the pointer seam, request_id propagation, validation failure propagated from creator as E_INVALID_INPUT (handler delegates validation to the store-layer orchestrator), strict-decode unknown-field rejection BEFORE creator runs, unauthenticated 401, role-deny 403 (handler never reached), cross-tenant 404 with principal home org forwarded, duplicate-id Conflict 409, store-unavailable 503, nil-creator typed Internal 500. Policy matrix test surface mirrors service_domains_create_policy_test.go one-for-one. Wiring footprint: adds ONE new positional arg (ServiceXxxCreator) to newRouteTable (~slot 57) and NewHandler (~slot 60); ~70 test files / ~120 call sites need a `fakeServiceXxxCreator{}` insertion via bulk substitution `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` -> `fakeServiceYyyReader{}, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, fakeServiceZzzReader{}` (where Yyy is the sibling Reader and Zzz is the next port after the new slot in the signature); the special cases are the in-package contract-test helper for the new resource (`reader` -> `..., reader, fakeServiceXxxCreator{}, ...`) and any sibling test where the local `reader` variable is bound to a DIFFERENT port type that happens to share an adjacent slot (e.g. service_variables_test.go's `reader` is a ServiceVariableReader, NOT a backup reader, so its slot ordering needs a separate substitution). Re-runnable via a small Python script that pattern-matches and applies the three substitutions in order — see the BE-0232+0233+0234 description above for the ast-walking variant if a new arg needs to be inserted at a structural offset rather than between two named neighbours.
ralph/progress.txt:257:- Verb-on-child-resource endpoint pattern (BE-0253+0254+0255, POST /v1/services/{service_id}/backups/{backup_id}/run; sibling of the services_restart verb-on-parent shape, but adds a tenant-scoped child existence check; clonable for any future "trigger an action on a worker-driven child resource" endpoint — backup PATCH/DELETE, future preview-run, future deployment-retry triggers): the store-layer composition is the canonical run-unit-of-work shape — XxxRepository.GetByID (composite (org, parent, child_id) read; pgx.ErrNoRows -> apierr.NotFound(kind, child_id) — the kind/id NEVER contains the org or parent so the customer-facing message stays free of identifiers the principal cannot already prove they own) + XxxRepository.MarkPending (UPDATE...RETURNING flipping the worker-observable status column; the bump_version trigger from the resource's migration owns version, so MarkPending NEVER writes it itself — preserves optimistic-concurrency invariants for future If-Match callers) + RunXxxInput (plain-string actor/correlation seam — no policy/telemetry import in the store package) + XxxService.Run orchestrator (validate IDs before Tx; in Tx: parent-resource tenant-scoped Get -> child-resource tenant-scoped Get -> precondition 409s on closed-taxonomy state (enabled=false, status=running) -> in-tx Authorize (defense-in-depth against grant-change races; on the SAME *Tx as the write) -> MarkPending -> audit append with status_before/status_after metadata; ALL state mutations + audit commit atomically). The audit Metadata records ONLY closed-taxonomy enums (status_before/status_after) and structural identifiers (service_id); caller-controlled free text (display_name, schedule) is NOT recorded — Run does not mutate either and including them would expose a future redaction footgun. The httpapi layer is the canonical verb-handler shape — empty body, principal home org + path {parent_id} + path {child_id} forwarded to the runner, RequireAuth gates on the action's catalog mapping (backup.run -> CapDeploy), nil-runner guard runs after RequireAuth admits, 202 Accepted on success. Route entry's resolver is the bare-id resolver of the PARENT (serviceIDResolver), not the child — the resource scope pins to the parent's path identifier so scoped grants on parent siblings still get the engine's one-way covers() rule applied. The catalog mapping (CapDeploy vs CapWrite vs CapRead) is THE single load-bearing distinction in the policy matrix: CapDeploy widens the allow set to include CI (which holds CapRead+CapDeploy), so backup.run is allowed for CI keys even though backup.create (CapWrite) is denied — the policy matrix test pins this distinction at every grant scope (project-, env-, service-, org-level grants) AND at the engine's cross-tenant deny (CapDeploy is OUTSIDE the support cross-tenant exception, same as CapWrite). The bulk fake-fixture sweep for the new positional port follows the BE-0244 pattern: `perl -i -pe 's/fakePREVIOUS\{\},/fakePREVIOUS{}, fakeNEW{},/g' $(grep -rl 'fakePREVIOUS{}' internal/controlplane/httpapi/)` keyed on the immediately preceding port's bare struct literal — 71 files in the BE-0253 sweep — with two always-manual follow-ups: (a) the resource's own create-test helper that uses a local variable `creator` for the previous slot, (b) any wiring test that explicitly passes `nil` for the previous slot to reach the typed-internal guard.
ralph/progress.txt:264:- Dimensional (magnitude-axis) quota dimension on a Create unit of work (BE-0331 pattern, internal/controlplane/store/projectservice.go QuotaReserver port + internal/controlplane/quota/quota.go Checker + internal/controlplane/store/serviceservice.go serviceCPUMillicoresByKind + internal/controlplane/store/service_quota_cpu_millicores_concurrent_test.go; structural template for BE-0332 memory_mb / BE-0333 storage_gb / and every future quota dimension whose allocation is a numeric magnitude per request rather than a +1 count). The FIRST dimensional story widens the load-bearing seam ONCE for the entire dimensional-quota family: the store-layer QuotaReserver interface gains a second method `ReserveAmount(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string, amount int64) error`, and the real adapter quota.Checker implements it as the canonical lock + sum-active + insert-reservation path with the amount parameterised. The count-axis convenience `Reserve(ctx, tx, orgID, resource string) error` is refactored to a one-line delegate `return c.ReserveAmount(...,1)`, so every prior count-axis call site is byte-identical and proves the new path is structurally compatible with the old. amount<=0 is rejected as Internal (wiring error — release is a separate path, not a negative reservation). The Checker validates amount, plan, resource, and limit BEFORE acquiring any row lock so a bad amount cannot block another tenant. The unit-of-work call site (ServiceService.Create) sources the per-request magnitude from a per-Kind constant map (serviceCPUMillicoresByKind: application=250, compose=500, database=500) via a `cpuMillicoresForServiceKind(kind)` helper — the map lives at the store layer so the dependency graph does not reach into the dokploy renderer's tier-aware defaults. The ReserveAmount call runs in the SAME *Tx as the count-axis Reserve calls, the desired-state write, the job enqueue, and the audit append; ordering is "every count-axis Reserve first, then the dimensional ReserveAmount" so a tenant who simultaneously exhausts a count dimension AND the dimensional dimension sees the count-dimension Resource surface in ExceededDetail (the LAST Reserve that fires produces the error). Three fakes (noopQuotaReserver in cmd/yalla-api/main.go, nopQuotaReserver in store/internal_test.go, recordingQuota in store/projectservice_test.go) need a one-line ReserveAmount addition to satisfy the widened interface; no other fakes exist because the concurrent tests use the real Checker. Test pattern: clone the BE-0326 concurrent test, replace the count-axis assertions with magnitude-axis ones — (1) policy LIMIT is a multiple of the per-Kind magnitude (cpu_millicores=1000 = 4 × 250-millicore apps); (2) expectedSuccesses = limit / per-Kind-magnitude; (3) detail.Requested is asserted to equal the per-Kind magnitude (NOT 1) — a regression hard-coding it back to 1 fails closed here; (4) detail.Reserved is expectedSuccesses × magnitude; (5) the active-reservations SUM(amount) assertion replaces / complements COUNT(*) — COUNT alone cannot tell whether the magnitude reached the reservation row Amount column, SUM(amount) closes the gap. A second non-concurrent test creates services across multiple kinds (application=250, database=500), exhausts the dimension exactly, then proves both a compose-kind request (would-reserve 500) and an application-kind request (would-reserve 250) are rejected — and that the two rejections report DIFFERENT detail.Requested values. The single equality check `composeDetail.Requested != appDetail.Requested` is the cheapest possible proof the per-Kind map actually fans out instead of collapsing to one constant. A SQL GROUP BY amount on quota_reservations confirms the magnitude distribution end-to-end (2 rows at 250 + 1 row at 500 for the canonical fixture). After BE-0331, EVERY future dimensional quota story (BE-0332 memory_mb, BE-0333 storage_gb, BE-0335 monthly_deployments) only needs a per-Kind/per-trigger magnitude helper and ONE ReserveAmount call site — the port is already widened and the real Checker implementation does not need to change again. The Reserve(...) call site convention stays for every count-axis dimension; ReserveAmount(...) is reserved for dimensions whose amount actually varies per request.
ralph/progress.txt:265:- BE-0332 memory_mb is the SECOND instance of the dimensional-quota template (BE-0331) and confirms the template clones without any port widening, Checker change, or fake update — only (a) a per-Kind magnitude map + helper in serviceservice.go (serviceMemoryMBByKind: application=512, compose=1024, database=1024), (b) one additional ReserveAmount call site inside ServiceService.Create AFTER the prior dimensional reservation, and (c) a structural clone of the concurrent integration test file with resource constant / magnitudes / limit swapped (service_quota_memory_mb_concurrent_test.go). Two load-bearing additions the template now records explicitly: (1) reservation order is part of the diagnostic contract — the LAST Reserve that fires produces the ExceededDetail.Resource surfaced to the caller, so a clone must seed the prior dimensional dimension(s) LOOSE in its concurrent test if it wants its own Resource to be the one that surfaces; a regression that reorders the reservations is detected by the clone test failing with the wrong detail.Resource (cpu_millicores instead of memory_mb, or vice versa). (2) The combined concurrent regression sweep across all dimensional tests requires `go test -race -parallel=2` (or lower) — two tests at 20 goroutines each saturate Postgres max_connections under default parallelism and surface as E_UNAVAILABLE instead of E_QUOTA_EXCEEDED. The per-test focused run remains green at full parallelism; this is a test-harness contention concern, not a code defect. Any future dimensional-dimension story (BE-0333 storage_gb / BE-0335 monthly_deployments / etc.) adds another concurrent test to the family and inherits both rules.
ralph/progress.txt:266:- Quota-dimension story whose call site is NOT yet wired (BE-0337 api_keys pattern, internal/controlplane/store/apikeyservice.go + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0027_quota_resource_api_keys.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/apikey_quota_api_keys_concurrent_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go; structural template for BE-0338 members and ANY future dimension story whose owning Service.Create predates the QuotaReserver port — applies whenever a dimension story is the FIRST to wire its service-layer Reserve, distinct from the BE-0331..BE-0336 'call site already there' siblings whose owning unit-of-work already reserves the dimension). The minimum atomic delivery is FOUR change axes: (1) closed-set expansion — a new `NNNN_quota_resource_<name>.{up,down}.sql` migration that follows the BE-0326 backup_schedules template verbatim (DO block looking up the inline DOMAIN CHECK by `contypid = 'quota_resource'::regtype AND contype = 'c'` so the migration is robust to any Postgres-assigned constraint name, then `ALTER DOMAIN quota_resource ADD CONSTRAINT quota_resource_check CHECK (VALUE IN (...))` with the prior set + the new label; the .down.sql is structurally identical and narrows back to the prior set, accepting that any row carrying the new label fails the restored CHECK — that is the correct behaviour, silently dropping rows would lose audit-relevant state); plus the matching `QuotaResource<Name>` constant + membership-set entry in internal/controlplane/store/quota.go; plus updating the `valid` slice in internal/controlplane/store/quota_internal_test.go::TestQuotaResourceValid which pins `len(valid) == len(quotaResources)` — the test is a sealing-trap that catches a forgotten membership-set entry but ALSO fires on a forgotten valid-slice entry; both must change together. (2) Constructor port widening — the existing service struct grows a `quota QuotaReserver` field, the constructor parameter list grows the same port, the nil-guard switch grows a `case quota == nil` arm placed BETWEEN the last existing repo arm and the audit arm (the convention is store → parent repos → own repo → authz? → quota → jobs? → audit), and the Create unit of work grows a `svc.quota.Reserve(ctx, tx, key.OrganizationID, string(QuotaResource<Name>))` call placed AFTER every existence/parent-lookup check and BEFORE the desired-state Insert so a 404 on the parent is never disguised as a quota rejection AND a row-level conflict (slug, prefix, unique constraint) rolls the reservation back with the rest of the unit of work. (3) Constructor call-site fanout — three call sites change: cmd/yalla-api/main.go grows a `<name>Quota := noopQuotaReserver{}` placeholder (the real quota.Checker adapter is wired separately by a cross-cutting plan-resolver story; placeholder mirrors the projectQuota / environmentQuota / serviceQuota convention exactly), the in-package `package store` constructor-nil test grows a `nopQuotaReserver{}` local + a new `{nil quota, ...}` case wedged BETWEEN the last existing nil case and the audit case (positional ordering must mirror the constructor's nil-guard switch), and the `package store_test` integration-test helper grows the shared `&recordingQuota{}` (defined in projectservice_test.go — not a local `noopQuotaReserver{}`, which is only visible to cmd/yalla-api; the store_test package has its OWN allow-all fake hierarchy and the convention is to reuse `recordingQuota` for happy-path constructors). (4) Concurrent integration test — clone of the BE-0336 concurrent_deployments test with five structural simplifications when the owning service has no Authorizer / JobEnqueuer ports (api_keys is pure Yalla identity with no Dokploy mirror, so APIKeyService takes neither): no `atomicAuthorizer` / `atomicJobs` fakes need to be defined or counted; no `authz.calls == attempts` and no `jobs.calls == successes` assertions; the per-goroutine input loop calls `auth.Generate()` INSIDE the goroutine so every concurrent attempt mints its own unique (prefix, secret_hash) pair via crypto/rand (the prefix column is UNIQUE so a shared pair would deterministically conflict and mask the quota race); the parent-row seed is only an `organizations` row (no project/environment/service chain — api_keys live directly under the tenant); and the leaky-input candidates in the rejection-message scan use the resource-specific prefix (`key-%02d` / `req_apikeys_%02d` / `corr_apikeys_%02d`) so an echoed input still fails the message guard. The 'second test' sibling (TestXxxCreateAppliesXxxQuotaIndependentlyOfOtherDimensions) seeds ONLY the dimension under enforcement, exhausts it sequentially, asserts a SIXTH Create surfaces the dimension-specific rejection, and asserts no reservation row exists on TWO unrelated dimensions (services / projects) — the symmetry guard that catches a future regression that drops the Reserve call OR accidentally chose a different resource string. The whole pattern compiles + passes -race against a real Postgres without touching ANY of the 70+ httpapi fake-fixture call sites because the service's HTTP layer is owned by a separate seam (the api_keys handler already calls APIKeyService.Create with the same input shape; only the store-layer wiring changes). Pre-existing test failures gotcha: TestAPIKeyService* and TestEnvironmentService* / TestOrganizationVariableServicePatch* fail on HEAD due to testutil.Factory id-prefix vs ParseSlug drift — confirmed by stashing the BE-0337 changes and reproducing the identical 23-test set on the base commit. NOT introduced by this pattern, owed to a focused triage story before BE-0500+ verification stories run.
